liblink
-------

liblink contains the runtime object linker used by GHC. The Cabal package is
named liblink; the native library is named link. Native clients link with
-llink (liblink.a or the platform's shared-library equivalent).

The current extraction preserves the RTS linker interface through forwarding
functions in rts/LinkerCompat.c. The headers under include/liblink provide the
compatibility and RTS friend interfaces; they are internal interfaces, not a
stable public embedding API. Unload checking and the GC remain in the RTS.

The installed liblink.h declares the handle API. Its current entry points are
unsupported stubs: ll_new returns NULL, and functions returning ll_status
return LL_E_UNSUPPORTED. RTS clients continue to use the compatibility
interface.

Object format dispatch
----------------------

Relocatable objects select a private format vtable from their input bytes before
backend initialization. ELF, Mach-O, and PE each own a typed immutable operation
table; selection uses an immutable registry of compiled implementations. The
selection stays with the object through resolution,
initialization, and disposal, even when the backend releases the input image.
Verification still belongs to the selected backend. Recognized ELF, Mach-O,
COFF, and Wasm input without an available backend returns LL_E_UNSUPPORTED;
unrecognized input returns LL_E_FORMAT. By default the registry contains the
native ELF, Mach-O, or PE backend. Cabal's +all-backends flag includes all three
implementations in the registry without changing native format identity or OS
services. This does not add a Wasm backend or implement the public handle API.
Archive traversal retains its existing member prefilter; members that
it loads use the same selection and initialization path as standalone objects.

Native shared libraries retain their OS loader path and have no static format
vtable. Static finalizers still run only after successful initialization, then
__cxa_finalize runs before format metadata, image, and section storage are freed.

When symbol extras replace an image with a contiguous mapping, the old image
storage is released after copying: mapped images are unmapped; heap images
release their original allocation base, accounting for stored misalignment.
A failed replacement leaves the original image and its ownership intact.

The shared extras allocator takes its payload stride and image-metadata refresh
callback from the backend. It records the stride with the object for protection
and cache lengths. A zero payload width retains its symbol count and allocation
path; refreshing moved image metadata still precedes publishing those fields.

Target architecture selection
-----------------------------

Private backend code uses ll_target.h for object architecture, word class,
and format-specific GOT/PLT and symbol-extra capabilities. A translation unit
may set LL_TARGET_ARCH to an LL_ARCH_* constant; otherwise it uses the
configured host architecture. Generated ll_platform.h always describes the
running host. OS loading, mapping, and native TLS services retain host guards.
Native format identity is independent of the compiled registry's order.

Symbol-extra records have named fixed-width x86_64, ARM, RISC-V, and PowerPC
layouts. Native compatibility clients retain their existing SymbolExtra alias;
PE's x86_64 helper explicitly uses its target layout. AArch64's legacy native
zero-sized representation and allocation/count behavior are retained while
the remaining shared storage and allocator interfaces are separated.

The private ll_build.h inclusion flags are separate from target selection and
native format identity. Shared ObjectCode storage and its ELF sentinel do not
depend on these private flags. Symbol-extra provenance distinguishes the shared
allocator from PE's separate M32 storage; only shared extras use the common
protector. ELF-only GOT lookup uses the object's recorded format identity,
which remains valid when tests copy an operation table.

All-backends qualification is pending. Current complete compile/link checks
target x86_64 Linux and Windows. Mach-O requires x86_64 or AArch64. Windows
foreign ELF and Mach-O remain unqualified for foreign runtime execution. Compiling
a foreign backend does not establish that foreign code can safely execute with
the running process's ABI or that its process services are available.

The first ELF x86_64 relocation slice now uses the private ll_reloc_ops.apply
table. Site stores use ll_site.work; PC-relative arithmetic uses ll_site.exec.
The synchronous call borrows an immutable ll_reloc_context containing the
referencing file's original symbol index, existing diagnostic name and raw
symbol type. This explicitly approved amendment to the initial internal
contract preserves extra-slot identity, STT_FUNC fallback eligibility and
exact diagnostics. The context lives in a private header and leaves the public
handle API unchanged.

Section.exec_start and ObjectCode.symbol_extras_exec record execution addresses
separately from storage. addSection and shared extras allocation initialize
them to their native work addresses. The paired x64 extras emitter writes the
original payload through work and returns exec for GOT/island arithmetic;
native pointer emitters retain
their existing behavior. All these fields are unconditional in the RTS friend
layout, so the RTS and library must be rebuilt together.

Normal ELF work and execution addresses still coincide. Payloads, symbol
lookup, range policy and diagnostics are preserved.

Private x64 ELF NO_EXEC mode
----------------------------

The private compatibility configuration accepts LL_CFG_NO_EXEC, an explicit
nonzero noexec_base, and LL_CFG_ALL_FORMATS when loading a non-native format.
This currently supports x86_64 ELF on a 64-bit host with that target/backend
compiled in. Other targets/formats return LL_E_UNSUPPORTED in this mode.
The installed handle API remains unsupported until WP2.9; these private
configuration fields are immutable after host installation.

Sections, BSS, COMMON, the GOT and symbol extras use separate zeroed heap
allocations with recorded allocation bases. Execution ranges advance from
noexec_base using a fixed 4096-byte x64 page size, independent of the host's
page size. Requested larger alignments are honored. Zero-sized blocks reserve
one byte and a distinct synthetic page. Addresses are monotonic and are not
recycled on unload. A failed individual allocation leaves its output and the
next address unchanged; a failed multi-block load can consume earlier successful
ranges. Storage and address-space exhaustion return LL_E_NOMEM.

Defined/local/COMMON symbols and GOT/island destinations use execution
addresses; payload stores use heap work addresses. Only explicit owned symbol
definitions participate in fallback lookup. NO_EXEC skips built-in population,
process/native lookup, native loading, executable mappings, memory protection,
cache flushing, and initializer/finalizer hooks and calls. Successful resolution
reaches OBJECT_READY without setting initializersRan. TLSGD and GOTTPOFF require
host TLS services and return LL_E_UNSUPPORTED before lookup in this mode.

The unit driver checks the actual core path, partial allocation cleanup and
native-service guards. Independent NO_EXEC goldens check deterministic
relocated section/extras/GOT bytes and execution addresses at base0x10000000;
normal-mode goldens remain unchanged. The ELF verifier bounds extras using
that same target payload width, with zero-width storage handled without
count division. This separately approved repair removes the native ARM64
zero-width division and keeps nonzero native widths unchanged. Other backend
conversions, two-host golden qualification and execution qualification remain
WP2.8 work.

The next AArch64 ELF conversion has normal-address baselines before any
relocation code changes: 20 layouts and 27 fields cover the thirteen ordinary
forms, negative displacements, direct/far branches, veneer identity/reuse,
and equal-valued symbols with distinct GOT slots. These data-only captures
use an x64 host with the AArch64 target compiled in; they do not qualify
AArch64 execution or enable AArch64 NO_EXEC admission.


Locking
-------

On native platforms, linker locks use pthread mutexes or Windows SRW locks in
both threaded and non-threaded RTS builds. Assertion-enabled pthread builds
use error-checking mutexes. Initialization and shutdown require exclusive
access; the mutex helpers do not implement one-time initialization.

Wasm deliberately preserves the existing single-threaded RTS behavior:
initialization, shutdown, lock, unlock, and held-lock assertions are no-ops
when wasm32_HOST_ARCH is defined. This is an explicit exception to the native
always-on locking rule. It provides no synchronization and does not establish
support for concurrent Wasm linker calls. Supporting those calls requires a
Wasm mutex backend and validation of that backend.

The Wasm helper branch has focused compile and no-op checks. These checks do
not qualify a complete GHC/WASI build or runtime.

Memory protection failures
--------------------------

Final OS memory-protection failures report LL_E_IO. Allocation and
address-space reservation failures report LL_E_NOMEM. If final protection
fails, the compatibility linker permanently quarantines that object
instance and its transitive dependents. The first operation preserves its
original diagnostic; later lookup, relocation, and initialization attempts
for affected objects fail with LL_E_STATE. Optimistic linking cannot turn
this failure into a placeholder address. A failed M32 flush also prevents
further allocation or flushing through that allocator.

Global resolution fails while a quarantined object remains loaded. Lookup
of an already-resolved independent object remains available. Pending
initializers skip quarantined objects and preserve the order and host hooks
of healthy objects. Ordinary unresolved-symbol retries keep their existing
behavior. The reported object state remains its last normal lifecycle phase;
the quarantine marker is private and introduces no new enum value.

Unload all affected objects before reloading them. Their mappings and
dependency identities remain owned until normal unload and GC disposal,
including objects whose recursive symbol lookup failed before registration.
A failed load can therefore leave a quarantined object registered for
unloading. Freshly loaded instances start without the marker. Previously
returned raw pointers cannot be revoked by the linker.

Initializer failures
--------------------

A null .init_array entry returns LL_E_INIT and releases the acquired program
environment exactly once. A failed initializer backend quarantines the object
and its transitive dependents under the policy above. The lookup that triggered
initialization returns NULL. Earlier constructor side effects remain; repeated
lookup or resolve cannot replay the executed prefix. Unload all affected objects
before reloading them. Pending initializer and finalizer metadata stays owned
until disposal, and finalizers run only after successful initialization. ELF and
PE physical disposal release retained initializer and finalizer lists even when
initialization failed or never started.

Native load failure cleanup
----------------------------

After a final failed dlopen, the POSIX compatibility loader completes its load
hooks and copies the error before invoking the optional on_unload callback.
It then frees unregistered object metadata. The callback sees OBJECT_READY with
a NULL native handle. The caller owns the copied error. Metadata cleanup also
works when on_unload is absent.

After a successful dlopen, failure to discover native mappings through dlinfo
or dl_iterate_phdr keeps the library loaded and marks it non-unloadable. Normal
registration and batch completion still run once. Linker debug logging reports
the discovery failure; loading succeeds without an error allocation or retry.
Logical unload can release host state, but GC retains the native mapping, as
it does for the existing successful lazy-load fallback.

Windows M32 reservation ownership
---------------------------------

Windows M32 mappings keep reservation records outside page payloads. Pool
transfers preserve page ownership. Discarding pages uses MEM_DECOMMIT on
the page range, and releases their logical ownership even if decommit
fails. Only an ownerless reservation can use MEM_RELEASE, with its original
base and a zero size. A failed base release keeps its record for retry.
Allocation-granularity padding is recorded separately from usable pages.

Shutdown drains the free-page pool and releases eligible reservations. It
preserves reservations still owned by live object allocators; the existing
compatibility shutdown does not destroy those allocators. Their disposal
must precede terminal reservation cleanup. Cleanup preserves an existing
primary error, and a failed attempt to make a page writable cannot write a
free-list header into that page. Page payload offsets, the pool limit,
32-page batching, and address placement policy remain unchanged.

Native Windows execution in CI is required to validate these OS paths.
Darwin source compilation does not qualify their Windows behavior.

Mach-O file records and constants are declared in installed `liblink/macho.h`,
without Apple SDK includes. Private metadata uses those declarations explicitly;
only a native Mach-O build exposes the temporary `Stub` compatibility alias.
Mapping/protection and instruction-cache work remain in the existing host-service
helpers. This declaration split does not enable foreign backend execution or
change supported file byte order. See tests/unit/README.md for layout controls.

COFF object records/constants live in installed `liblink/coff.h`, with the
Windows two-byte symbol/relocation packing preserved. `PEi386Types.h` holds
SDK-free format metadata. `PEi386Host.c` owns DLL loading/caches, Windows symbol
lookup, loaded-module inspection, and native unwind registration. SDK handles,
calling conventions, and runtime-function records stay in that host layer.
`PEi386Services.h` gives the object backend typed private process-service calls;
a non-Windows host returns LL_E_UNSUPPORTED when those services are required.

The real PE object code can compile with LL_ALL_BACKENDS on another host.
Registry admission and execution policy remain separate integration work.
Native DLL/unwind call order and ownership are preserved; native Windows
runtime qualification remains required. The retained direct COFF string-table
count load assumes four-byte alignment. A normal one-data-symbol MinGW GCC
object violates that assumption; the separate repair remains unapplied pending
approval. Portable data controls use an explicitly aligned compiler fixture.

Backend host memory services
----------------------------

Portable backend code uses private host adapters for file opening, anonymous
section allocation and read-only GOT protection. POSIX calls retain their
existing flags, access modes, error messages and cleanup order. Windows paths
use the existing wide-path rts-fs service. Anonymous sections use the existing
VirtualAlloc host service and SECTION_MMAP disposal releases that reservation.
File-backed section mapping returns LL_E_UNSUPPORTED on Windows; the normal
Windows compatibility loader supplies heap images instead of mapped files.

The GOT protection adapter returns the OS result without setting a linker
error. Its caller retains the legacy log-and-continue behavior on protection
failure. Changing that policy requires a separate reviewed behavior change.

RISC-V cache completion
-----------------------

The standalone RISC-V cache-flush helper uses a C11 sequentially consistent
fence after its existing flush calls, independent of the consuming RTS way.
This retains the original threaded fence and adds that ordering for consumers
whose non-threaded RTS macro was empty. Cache ranges and relocation arithmetic
are unchanged. Compiler controls do not replace native RISC-V qualification.
